add("what is feature flags and feature management?","Feature flags (feature toggles) decouple deployment from release. Deploy code to production with features hidden behind flags. Enable for specific users, gradually roll out, or quickly disable if issues arise. Types: Release toggles (ship incomplete features safely), Experiment toggles (A/B testing), Ops toggles (circuit breakers, kill switches), Permission toggles (premium features). Implementation: simple if/else with config, or use feature flag services (LaunchDarkly, Unleash, Flagsmith, PostHog). Best practices: keep flags short-lived (remove after rollout complete), avoid flag dependencies (flag A depends on flag B), maintain flag inventory, clean up stale flags. Use cases: canary releases (enable for 1% of users → 10% → 50% → 100%), beta features (enable for specific user groups), kill switches (disable failing features without deployment), A/B testing (measure impact of changes). Technical implementation: server-side evaluation (flag service returns value), client-side SDKs (evaluate locally for speed). Feature flags enable trunk-based development — everyone works on main, features are hidden until ready. This reduces merge conflicts, enables faster CI/CD, and allows product teams to control releases independently from engineering deployments.","Feature flags, toggles, canary release, A/B testing, LaunchDarkly, trunk-based, rollout.")

add("what is DNS and how does domain resolution work?","DNS (Domain Name System) translates human-readable domain names to IP addresses. Like a phone book for the internet. Resolution process: 1) Browser checks its cache. 2) OS checks its cache (/etc/hosts file, OS DNS cache). 3) Query recursive resolver (usually ISP or 8.8.8.8/1.1.1.1). 4) Resolver checks its cache. If not cached: 5) Query root nameserver (13 clusters worldwide, knows TLD servers). 6) Root says 'ask .com TLD server.' 7) Query TLD nameserver (.com, .org, .io). 8) TLD says 'ask ns1.example.com (authoritative nameserver).' 9) Query authoritative nameserver — returns the IP address. Record types: A (IPv4 address), AAAA (IPv6), CNAME (alias to another domain), MX (mail server), TXT (text data — SPF, DKIM, domain verification), NS (nameserver), SOA (zone authority). TTL (Time To Live): how long to cache the record. Short TTL (60s) for frequently changing IPs, long TTL (86400s) for stable records. Common DNS providers: Cloudflare DNS, Route 53 (AWS), Google Cloud DNS. DNS can be used for: load balancing (multiple A records, round-robin), CDN routing (GeoDNS — return closest server), failover (health checks, automatic DNS failover). Security: DNSSEC (sign DNS records to prevent tampering), DNS-over-HTTPS (DoH) for privacy.","DNS, domain resolution, A record, CNAME, MX, TTL, nameserver, DNSSEC, recursive resolver.")

add("what is load balancing strategies?","Load balancing distributes traffic across multiple servers to ensure reliability and performance. Algorithms: Round Robin — requests go to servers in order. Simple but doesn't account for server capacity or current load. Weighted Round Robin — assign weights based on server capacity. More powerful servers get more traffic. Least Connections — route to server with fewest active connections. Good when request processing time varies. IP Hash — hash client IP to determine server. Ensures same client always hits same server (sticky sessions). Useful for session-based applications. Least Response Time — route to server with fastest response + fewest connections. Random — simple, surprisingly effective at scale with many servers. Types: Layer 4 (Transport) — routes based on IP and port. Fast, doesn't inspect content. TCP/UDP level. Layer 7 (Application) — routes based on HTTP headers, URL path, cookies. Can make intelligent routing decisions. Slower but more flexible. Tools: Nginx (L7, most popular reverse proxy/LB), HAProxy (L4/L7, high performance), AWS ALB (L7, managed), AWS NLB (L4, managed), Traefik (cloud-native, auto-discovery). Health checks: periodically verify backend servers are healthy. Remove unhealthy servers from rotation, re-add when recovered. Session persistence: sticky sessions (always route to same server), or store sessions externally (Redis) for true stateless load balancing.","Load balancing, round robin, least connections, Layer 4, Layer 7, Nginx, HAProxy, ALB.")

add("what is database replication and sharding?","Replication and sharding scale databases beyond a single server. Replication: copy data across multiple servers. Leader-Follower (Master-Slave): one leader handles writes, followers handle reads. Read scalability. If leader fails, promote a follower (failover). Replication lag: followers may be slightly behind leader (eventual consistency). Leader-Leader (Multi-Master): multiple nodes accept writes. Complex conflict resolution. Used when writes need to be geographically distributed. Synchronous vs Asynchronous: synchronous (wait for follower confirmation — guaranteed consistency, higher latency), asynchronous (don't wait — faster, risk of data loss on leader failure). Sharding (Horizontal Partitioning): split data across multiple databases. Each shard holds a subset of data. Shard key determines which shard stores each record. Strategies: range-based (users A-M on shard 1, N-Z on shard 2 — uneven distribution risk), hash-based (hash(user_id) % num_shards — even distribution, harder to range query), directory-based (lookup table maps data to shards — flexible but adds a hop). Challenges: cross-shard queries (joins across shards are expensive), rebalancing (adding/removing shards requires data migration), consistent hashing (minimizes data movement when shards change), application complexity (application needs to know which shard to query). When to shard: only when necessary. Start with read replicas, then vertical scaling, connection pooling, query optimization. Shard as last resort.","Replication, sharding, leader-follower, consistent hashing, partition, read replica, failover.")

add("what is software testing anti-patterns?","Common testing mistakes that reduce test effectiveness. Testing implementation details — coupling tests to internal structure means any refactor breaks tests. Test behavior: 'when I add item to cart, total updates' not 'when addItem called, cartItems array has length 1.' Too many mocks — mocking everything means you're not testing real behavior. Prefer integration tests with real dependencies when possible. Test doubles should be at boundaries (external APIs, databases). Shared mutable state — tests that depend on order or modify shared state. Each test should set up its own data and be independent. Flaky tests — tests that sometimes pass and sometimes fail. Causes: timing issues, shared state, external dependencies. Fix immediately — flaky tests erode trust in the test suite. Testing getters/setters — trivial code doesn't need testing. Test logic, not language features. No assertions — tests that execute code but don't verify outcomes. Coverage looks good but tests prove nothing. Too much setup — if test setup is 50 lines and the actual test is 2 lines, something's wrong. Extract test helpers, use factories. Testing private methods — test public API only. Private methods are tested through public methods that use them. 100% coverage obsession — coverage measures lines executed, not correctness. 80% meaningful coverage is better than 100% superficial coverage. Slow tests — slow test suites don't get run often. Keep unit tests fast (under 10ms each).","Testing anti-patterns, mocking, flaky tests, coverage, implementation details, test behavior.")

add("what is web accessibility testing?","Accessibility testing ensures your website works for everyone including people with disabilities. Automated testing (catches ~30% of issues): Lighthouse accessibility audit (Chrome DevTools), axe DevTools browser extension, Pa11y (CLI tool), eslint-plugin-jsx-a11y (catch issues during development). Manual testing: keyboard navigation (can you tab through all interactive elements? Is focus visible? Can you operate all controls without a mouse?), screen reader testing (NVDA on Windows, VoiceOver on macOS/iOS, TalkBack on Android — does content make sense when read aloud?), zoom testing (is content usable at 200% zoom?), color contrast (4.5:1 ratio for normal text, 3:1 for large text — use WebAIM contrast checker). Common issues: images without alt text, form inputs without labels, insufficient color contrast, missing focus indicators, non-semantic HTML (div with onclick instead of button), missing skip navigation link, auto-playing media without controls, inaccessible modals (focus trap, escape to close). WCAG 2.1 AA is the standard target. ARIA usage: use semantic HTML first (button, nav, main, aside). ARIA is for cases where HTML semantics aren't sufficient — aria-label, aria-describedby, aria-live for dynamic content, role when no HTML element fits. Testing checklist: keyboard navigation, screen reader, color contrast, zoom, reduced motion preference, form accessibility.","Accessibility testing, WCAG, screen reader, keyboard navigation, ARIA, color contrast, axe.")

add("what is error handling patterns?","Error handling strategies for robust applications. Fail fast — detect errors early, fail immediately with clear messages. Don't let invalid state propagate. Validate inputs at boundaries (API endpoints, form handlers). Use early returns: if (!isValid) throw new Error('Invalid input'). Result types — instead of throwing exceptions, return Result<T, Error>. Rust's Result enum, Go's multiple return values, TypeScript with discriminated unions. Makes error paths explicit — caller must handle both success and failure. Error boundaries (React) — catch rendering errors in component tree. Display fallback UI instead of crashing entire app. Don't use for event handlers (use try/catch there). Retry with backoff — transient failures (network timeouts, rate limits) often succeed on retry. Exponential backoff: wait 1s, 2s, 4s, 8s. Add jitter (random delay) to prevent thundering herd. Max retries to prevent infinite loops. Circuit breaker — when a service is failing, stop calling it temporarily. States: closed (normal), open (failing, return error immediately), half-open (try one request to check recovery). Prevents cascade failures. Global error handlers — catch unhandled errors at top level. process.on('uncaughtException') in Node, window.onerror in browser, error middleware in Express. Log errors with context (user ID, request ID, stack trace). Structured error responses — consistent error format: { error: { code: 'VALIDATION_ERROR', message: 'Email is required', field: 'email' } }.","Error handling, fail fast, Result type, circuit breaker, retry, error boundary, backoff.")

add("what is data serialization formats?","Data serialization converts data structures into a format for storage or transmission. JSON (JavaScript Object Notation): human-readable, widely supported, text-based. The standard for REST APIs, config files. Limitations: no comments, no date type, verbose, slow to parse for large data. Protocol Buffers (protobuf, Google): binary format, schema-defined (.proto files), much smaller and faster than JSON. Used in gRPC. Requires schema compilation. Not human-readable. MessagePack: binary JSON — smaller, faster, maintains JSON compatibility. Good for high-performance APIs that don't need human readability. YAML: superset of JSON, human-friendly with indentation-based structure. Used for config files (Docker Compose, Kubernetes manifests, GitHub Actions). Supports comments. Pitfall: indentation sensitivity, 'Norway problem' (NO interpreted as boolean). TOML: simple config format, less ambiguous than YAML. Used by Rust (Cargo.toml), Python (pyproject.toml). Avro: binary with schema evolution support. Used in Apache Kafka, data pipelines. Schema stored alongside data. XML: verbose, legacy. Still used in enterprise systems, SOAP APIs, Android layouts. Choose: JSON for APIs and interoperability, protobuf for high-performance internal services, YAML for configuration, TOML for simple config, Avro for data pipelines.","Serialization, JSON, protobuf, MessagePack, YAML, TOML, Avro, XML, binary format.")

add("what is Gorkem's banking-management-system project?","Gorkem's banking-management-system is a full-stack application that demonstrates software engineering fundamentals beyond AI/ML. The project covers: backend architecture (proper separation of concerns — controller, service, repository layers), database design (relational modeling — accounts, transactions, users, proper foreign key relationships), CRUD operations (create accounts, process transactions, query balances), business logic (transfer validation, balance checks, transaction history), authentication and authorization (user roles, secure access), API design (RESTful endpoints for banking operations). This project shows Gorkem's ability to build structured, well-architected applications — not just scripts or notebooks. Skills demonstrated: MVC/layered architecture, database schema design, input validation, error handling, transaction management (ACID properties in practice — bank transfers must be atomic), security awareness (handling financial data). Why it matters: many AI/ML engineers can build models but struggle with production software engineering. The banking system shows Gorkem can do both — build ML models AND build production-quality applications with proper architecture. This combination is increasingly valued as companies need ML engineers who can deploy and maintain systems, not just train models.","Gorkem, banking-management-system, full-stack, architecture, CRUD, database design, ACID.")

add("what is Gorkem's facelock project?","Gorkem's facelock project combines computer vision with practical security — face recognition for access control. Technical components: face detection (locating faces in images/video — using Haar cascades or deep learning-based detectors like MTCNN), face recognition (comparing detected faces against known faces — face embeddings using deep neural networks like FaceNet or dlib), real-time processing (webcam feed processing, frame-by-frame analysis), access control logic (match/no-match decision, threshold tuning for false positive/negative trade-off). The pipeline: capture frame → detect faces → extract face embeddings (128-dimensional vector representing the face) → compare against database of known faces → if distance below threshold, grant access. Key challenges: lighting variation (face looks different in different lighting), angle variation (frontal vs profile), real-time performance (processing must be fast enough for smooth UX), spoofing prevention (photo vs real face — liveness detection). This project connects to Gorkem's broader CV portfolio — yolo-custom-detector for object detection, facelock for face recognition. Together they demonstrate competence across multiple computer vision tasks. Practical applications: office access control, attendance systems, device unlock. Combines Gorkem's ML expertise with security awareness from phishing-domain-detection.","Gorkem, facelock, face recognition, computer vision, embeddings, access control, real-time.")

add("what is distributed tracing?","Distributed tracing tracks requests as they flow through microservices. Essential for debugging in distributed systems. Concepts: Trace — the complete journey of a request across all services. Span — a single operation within a service (database query, API call, function execution). Each span has: trace ID (shared across all spans in a trace), span ID (unique), parent span ID (which span triggered this one), start/end timestamps, metadata (tags, logs). How it works: when a request enters the system, generate a trace ID. Pass this ID in headers (traceparent) to every service the request touches. Each service creates spans for its operations, all linked by the trace ID. Visualization: waterfall diagram shows the complete request timeline — which services were called, how long each took, where bottlenecks are. Tools: Jaeger (open source, by Uber), Zipkin (open source, by Twitter), AWS X-Ray (managed), Datadog APM, Honeycomb. OpenTelemetry: vendor-neutral standard for instrumentation. SDKs for all major languages. Auto-instrumentation for common frameworks (Express, Django, Spring). Exports to any backend (Jaeger, Zipkin, Datadog). Use cases: latency investigation (which service is slow?), error tracking (where did the error originate?), dependency mapping (which services talk to each other?), performance optimization (identify bottlenecks). Essential for any microservices architecture.","Distributed tracing, Jaeger, Zipkin, OpenTelemetry, span, trace, microservices, debugging.")

add("what is Git hooks and automation?","Git hooks are scripts that run automatically at specific points in the Git workflow. Client-side hooks: pre-commit — runs before commit is created. Use for: linting (ESLint, Prettier), running tests, checking for secrets (detect-secrets), formatting code. If the hook exits with non-zero status, the commit is rejected. commit-msg — validate or modify commit message. Enforce conventional commits format (feat:, fix:, docs:). prepare-commit-msg — modify default commit message template. pre-push — runs before push. Use for: running full test suite, checking branch naming conventions. post-checkout — runs after checkout. Use for: installing dependencies when switching branches. Server-side hooks: pre-receive, update, post-receive — run on the server. Enforce policies (no force push to main, require signed commits). Tools: Husky — easy Git hooks setup for Node.js projects. Install hooks via package.json. lint-staged — run linters only on staged files (not the entire codebase). Much faster for large projects. commitlint — validate commit messages against conventional commits. Commitizen — interactive conventional commit message generator. Example workflow: developer commits → pre-commit hook runs Prettier + ESLint on staged files → commit-msg hook validates message format → if all pass, commit is created. This automation enforces code quality without manual effort.","Git hooks, Husky, lint-staged, pre-commit, commitlint, conventional commits, automation.")

add("what is API gateway pattern?","API Gateway is a single entry point for all client requests to backend services. Acts as reverse proxy, routing requests to appropriate microservices. Functions: routing (map external URLs to internal services), authentication/authorization (verify JWT tokens, API keys before forwarding), rate limiting (protect backend from abuse), request/response transformation (aggregate data from multiple services, transform formats), load balancing (distribute requests across service instances), caching (cache frequent responses at the gateway level), SSL termination (handle HTTPS at gateway, internal communication can be HTTP), monitoring/logging (centralized request logging, metrics). Patterns: simple proxy (route requests by path — /users → user service, /orders → order service), BFF (Backend for Frontend — separate gateways for web, mobile, IoT with different aggregation), aggregation (gateway combines responses from multiple services into single response). Tools: Kong (open source, plugin ecosystem), AWS API Gateway (managed, serverless integration), Nginx (reverse proxy with gateway capabilities), Traefik (cloud-native, auto-discovery from Docker/Kubernetes), Express Gateway (Node.js based), Envoy (service mesh sidecar proxy). Anti-patterns: putting business logic in the gateway (keep it thin), single point of failure (ensure high availability), becoming a bottleneck (scale gateway appropriately). API Gateway is essential for microservices but adds latency — ensure it's justified.","API gateway, routing, rate limiting, Kong, reverse proxy, BFF, authentication, aggregation.")

add("what is immutable infrastructure?","Immutable infrastructure means servers are never modified after deployment. Instead of updating software on existing servers (mutable), you create new servers with the updated software and replace the old ones. Traditional (mutable): SSH into server → apt update → deploy new code → restart service. Problem: configuration drift (servers diverge over time), hard to reproduce, 'works on my machine'. Immutable approach: build new machine image (AMI, Docker image) with all changes → deploy new instances with new image → route traffic to new instances → destroy old instances. Tools: Packer (build machine images), Docker (containerize applications), Terraform (provision infrastructure). Benefits: consistency (every server from same image is identical), reproducibility (rebuild any environment from image), rollback (just switch back to previous image), security (no SSH access needed, reduced attack surface). Implementation: golden images (bake application into AMI/VM image), containers (Docker images — most common approach today), serverless (no servers to manage at all). Blue-green deployment: maintain two identical environments. Deploy to blue, test, switch traffic from green to blue. If issues, switch back instantly. Canary deployment: route small percentage of traffic to new version. Monitor metrics. Gradually increase if healthy. Immutable infrastructure is a mindset shift — treat servers like cattle not pets. Don't repair, replace.","Immutable infrastructure, golden image, blue-green, canary, Docker, Packer, configuration drift.")

add("what is Elasticsearch and full-text search?","Elasticsearch is a distributed search and analytics engine built on Apache Lucene. Designed for full-text search, log analytics, and real-time data exploration. Core concepts: Index — like a database table. Collection of documents with similar characteristics. Document — JSON object stored in an index. Basic unit of information. Mapping — schema definition (field types, analyzers). Inverted index — maps terms to documents containing them. 'the quick brown fox' → {the: [doc1, doc3], quick: [doc1], brown: [doc1, doc2], fox: [doc1]}. This is why search is fast — lookup term → get document list. Analysis pipeline: character filters → tokenizer → token filters. Example: 'The Quick BROWN Fox' → lowercase → ['the', 'quick', 'brown', 'fox'] → remove stop words → ['quick', 'brown', 'fox']. Query types: match (full-text search with analysis), term (exact match, no analysis), bool (combine queries with must/should/must_not/filter), range (numeric/date ranges), fuzzy (typo tolerance). Relevance scoring: TF-IDF (term frequency × inverse document frequency) or BM25 (improved version, default). ELK Stack: Elasticsearch + Logstash (data ingestion) + Kibana (visualization). Common for log management. Alternatives: OpenSearch (AWS fork), Typesense (simpler), Meilisearch (developer-friendly), Algolia (managed SaaS). Use when: search is core feature, log analytics, complex text queries. For simple search: PostgreSQL full-text search is often sufficient.","Elasticsearch, full-text search, inverted index, Lucene, ELK, Kibana, BM25, analysis.")

add("what is Gorkem's education and background?","Gorkem Ergüne is a Computer Science student at Yeditepe University and an AI Product Developer. His education combines formal CS fundamentals with hands-on project-based learning. At Yeditepe, he studies core CS: algorithms, data structures, operating systems, databases, computer networks, discrete mathematics, software engineering — the theoretical foundation every engineer needs. But Gorkem goes beyond coursework with an impressive self-directed project portfolio: miniature-transformers (deep learning fundamentals), wiki2bpe (NLP fundamentals), gorkem-os (systems programming), banking-management-system (software engineering), yolo-custom-detector and facelock (computer vision), phishing-domain-detection (ML + security), NMT (machine translation). This combination — university education + practical projects — is the ideal engineering background. Theory without practice produces engineers who know concepts but can't build. Practice without theory produces engineers who build but don't know why things work. Gorkem also contributes to the community through his ayarlicazhocam bot, which guides people on engineering education, startup development, YKS strategies, and project guidance. His portfolio is available at gorkemergune.github.io. He approaches engineering with intellectual curiosity — building things from scratch (not just using libraries) to understand how they work at a fundamental level.","Gorkem, education, Yeditepe University, CS student, AI Product Developer, portfolio, projects.")

